{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"prosperous-busying-newt","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\n`RemoteRef` — the backend-neutral identity value `{vendor, instance, remote_id}` defined in\n`rebar_reconciler/_backend.py` — is **never constructed in production code**. Verified by grep across\nthe repo:\n\n* in `src/`: only the frozen dataclass definition (`_backend.py:60`) and docstring mentions;\n* the ONLY constructions anywhere are in tests — `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/backend_support.py:174`\n  (the FakeBackend helper) and `test_backend_contract.py:96-97` (the value-equality contract test).\n\nSo `RemoteRef` is port vocabulary that no code path populates. Its `instance` field is documented as\nwhat 'names the concrete deployment (e.g. a Jira site / project host) so two instances of the same\nvendor never collide'.\n\n## Why this matters now\n\nEpic e369 (Jira Server/Data Center support) rests on a shared-identity decision: DC-created tickets\ncarry the SHARED `jira` creation channel and `jira-` local-id prefix, **with the deployment\ndistinguished by `RemoteRef.instance`**. The first two halves are real and enforced. The third is\ncurrently aspirational — nothing populates `instance`, so two deployments of the same vendor are NOT\nin fact distinguished by it.\n\nThis surfaced in story J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1], whose AC required asserting 'a populated\n`RemoteRef.instance`' on a DC-created ticket. That assertion is not satisfiable against any reconcile\npass, because no pass builds a RemoteRef. J7's AC was narrowed to the provenance that IS observable\n(creation_channel + local-id prefix) with this ticket filed for the remainder, rather than asserting\nhalf a criterion and calling it met.\n\n## Scope sketch (needs design, not just implementation)\n\nDecide where a RemoteRef is minted on the inbound path and where `instance` is sourced from (the\nconfigured base URL / site host is the obvious candidate, but it must be STABLE across a URL change\nor it is not an identity). Then persist or thread it so a local ticket's remote binding carries the\ndeployment, and the collision the field exists to prevent is actually prevented.\n\n**Do not** treat this as a mechanical fill-in: if `instance` is derived from a mutable base URL, an\noperator moving their instance silently re-identifies every ticket. That tradeoff is the substance of\nthis ticket.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a reconcile pass constructs a RemoteRef with a populated, non-empty `instance`\n- [ ] two deployments of the same vendor produce DIFFERENT `instance` values, asserted by test\n- [ ] the chosen `instance` source is documented, including what happens when it changes\n- [ ] epic e369's shared-identity claim is either satisfied or its ADR amended to match reality","id":"6a91-7429-e521-4a2e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Populate RemoteRef on the inbound path so deployment identity is observable"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785469966147746001,"uuid":"f0c89068-81af-4429-8150-fc4037c81bc4"}